Four truths and their different tasks (@buddhist-truths-and-tasks: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @buddhism, #source @buddhist-source-noble-truths, #philosophy @buddhist-conditionality-and-cessation, #buddhism @buddhist-conditionality-and-cessation) { r_54ZMTWM29FACEZVHRG8WQPE6HF
**Read:** the introduction and complete anthology [Noble Truths, Noble Path](../sources/Noble-truths.lingua#r_370CHFFQS3H0YVWKB1RT1698WJ), following its chapter order. Read a chapter at a time, keeping the sutta numbers beside your notes. Return to the eightfold path when the anthology presupposes it.

Dukkha is often translated as suffering or unsatisfactoriness. Neither English word settles the range of its use. Follow the examples and explanations in the discourses. Distinguish understanding dukkha, abandoning its origin, realising cessation, and developing the path: these are different tasks, not four versions of believing a proposition.

**Reconstruct:** choose a discourse used by Bodhi and separate the translated teaching from his introductory explanation. State the relation it proposes between a difficulty, its condition, its ending, and cultivation. Locate an objection or uncertainty rather than smoothing it into a familiar slogan.

**Check:** explain why a statement about suffering is not by itself the entire diagnosis. Complete this anthology before moving to the full Saṃyutta collection.

**Continue:** [Conditionality, cessation, and what liberation means](Conditionality-and-cessation.lingua#r_5853A6MGKJC0D6TQDFHMM9B2K7).

**Return:** [Buddhism: from the path to sustained inquiry](../Buddhism.lingua#r_7P61Z6HHZNZZ8ND3DHXSFSNRRE).
